{
  "_why": [
    "A COUNT IS NOT A COMPOSITION, AND A NAME IS NOT A MEMBERSHIP.",
    "",
    "Two sets of the same size are indistinguishable by count, so a pin that only",
    "counts survives the swap that matters: delete a load-bearing refusal, add a",
    "benign passing test to the same module, and the total is unchanged.",
    "",
    "The FIRST version of this file pinned bare NAMES, and that was not enough --",
    "found by review (PR #108, P2). The checker discarded each testcase's module,",
    "so a required name could be satisfied by a DIFFERENT collected module while",
    "the per-module counts stayed right. Rename the pinned descriptor proof to a",
    "filler and hand its old name to an unpinned test in the revert-pins module,",
    "and the step passed with the proof gone. The change's own thesis failing on",
    "itself: what varies is the (module, name) PAIR, so that is what is pinned.",
    "",
    "`counts` guards collection loss. `required` is keyed BY MODULE and names the",
    "tests whose disappearance would be a silent loss of a refusal the project",
    "depends on, plus at least one POSITIVE CONTROL per step -- a step that pins",
    "only refusals is consistent with a build that refuses everything.",
    "",
    "`required` is a SUBSET, deliberately. Listing every name would churn on every",
    "added test and pin nothing extra: the count already catches deletion, and what",
    "`required` adds is resistance to SUBSTITUTION.",
    "",
    "Names are BASE function names. A parametrised test appears in JUnit as",
    "`name[param]`, and the checker matches on the base, so re-parametrising a test",
    "keeps the pin while deleting or moving it breaks it."
  ],
